president trump announces his nominee  today to fill the supreme court vacancy  left by the death of justice ruth bader  ginsburg  the president is expected to name amy  coney barrett a federal judge on the  seventh circuit court of appeals  if confirmed barrett will be the third  justice the president has placed on the  supreme court  giving conservatives a clear majority  the nomination comes a little more than  a week after ginsburg's death  on friday her body laid in state at the  u.s capitol  she was the first woman and the first  jewish person to hold that honor  it also comes less than six weeks before  the election which could shift control  of the white house and the senate  back to the democrats the nomination  sets up what could be a fierce  confirmation battle in the senate with  the election looming over the hearings  for more we're joined from washington by  bob cusack he's editor-in-chief of  the hill bob good morning to you you've  already established that the  scotus nomination here will be the  defining issue in this election with the  election  38 days away how important is this for  voters on election day  i don't think it's going to change many  votes but it certainly is going to shore  up the base the base of both the  republican party and the democratic  party because this is such a big deal  this will  really could set the stage on a number  of rulings ranging from  guns to abortion now most people have  already decided who they're going to be  voting for  about 80 percent of the country polls  show but  this is something that i think does help  the president it may endanger some  senate republicans who are in tough  races but this does unify the party and  that's important  for this white house because he has been  at odds with some republicans over the  last  three and a half years senator kamala  harris will be wearing several hats  at this at this hearing as the  democratic  vice presidential pick and as a member  of the judiciary committee so  what are we expecting to see from her  well harris is a former prosecutor  and she did very well on hearings with  uh kavanaugh and also  grilled attorney general bill barr  whether trump was asking him to do any  investigations and barr wasn't really  able to answer  that question he kind of stammered on it  so all eyes will be on harris we haven't  seen much of harris recently she hasn't  done a press conference  uh since she was named so i think this  is going to be a big stage for democrats  have to be a little bit careful not  going too far  and being too aggressive and make a  likely nominee  tony barrett look kind of sympathetic  according to our cbs polling bob trump  and biden neck and neck in big electoral  states like florida and  texas so with less than 40 days to go  before the election how do you expect  both candidates to approach that  well trump is going all out for florida  and absolutely uh trump needs to win  florida if  trump does not win florida uh he's not  going to win a second term so he's been  he's recently gave 8 billion to puerto  rico he recently announced a  prescription  drug plan for seniors that could help  him in florida it's very tight there and  trump also has to win texas he's favored  i think  slightly to win texas but that state has  been changing over the years those are  two basically  must win states uh for the president i  mean biden has to win certain states i  think he's got to win michigan he's  certainly got to win virginia  uh but for for trump uh it's all in for  florida  and bob the trump campaign was focusing  so much on wisconsin speaking of these  swing states and one that he won last  year now it seems like  they're they're focusing a little bit  more on pennsylvania what's the strategy  yeah  well biden has a lead in pennsylvania  but he has not been able to shake  the president uh in between pittsburgh  and philadelphia there there's a strong  trump base there so that is something  that i i think worries the biden  campaign that  that they have not been able to really  expand their lead because that is a  state that usually goes for democrats  but  trump won it in 2016 and that is a  battleground state we also don't know  some of these states including michigan  we may not know for several days after  the election who actually won  the state all right bob cusack my guess  is we'll see you again before the month  is out thanks very much again you can  see the first presidential debate right  here our coverage begins tuesday night  at 9 eastern 8 central on  cbs
 

